{"id":"indicator_selection_principle","topic":"indicator_selection","content":"Choose indicators because they operationalize a specific hypothesis or control a known risk, not because a charting convention is popular. Freeze a small set before testing; record all variants. Indicators derived from price are transformations of the same OHLC information, not independent confirmations. Evaluate incremental value by ablation and matched-coverage comparisons on chronological out-of-sample data after fees and slippage. The supplied research does not support any indicator combination as capable of reaching the user's targets."}
{"id":"hypothesis_A_indicator_map","topic":"session_momentum_indicators","content":"For session-momentum hypothesis A, use (a) RTH cumulative return from 09:30 ET to the fixed decision time; (b) first-30-minute return; (c) a same-clock standardized score using only prior sessions; optionally (d) realized volatility/volume as pre-registered conditioning variables. Do not use final-day high/low/close at an earlier decision time. Gao, Han, Li & Zhou, 'Market Intraday Momentum,' Journal of Financial Economics 129(2), 2018, 394-414, DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2018.05.009, reports that the first half-hour SPY return from the previous close predicts the final half-hour return in 1993-2013 data; reported predictive R-squared is modest and the instrument/horizon differ from 5-minute NQ. This motivates a falsifiable NQ replication, not a direct NQ profitability claim."}
{"id":"hypothesis_B_indicator_map","topic":"opening_range_and_relative_volume","content":"For opening-range breakout, compute ORH=max(high) and ORL=min(low) over the six 5-minute RTH bars 09:30-10:00 ET; freeze them after 10:00. Candidate confirmation: current bar volume divided by the average volume at that same time-of-day over prior sessions (RVOL). Require per-bar volume and a point-in-time historical baseline; OHLC alone cannot test the volume-confirmed hypothesis. A US-equity opening-range study by Zarattini, Barbon & Aziz is a working paper/preprint, not NQ futures evidence: SFI Research Paper 24-98A / SSRN 4729284, https://ssrn.com/abstract=4729284 and author-hosted institutional copy https://alexandria.unisg.ch/server/api/core/bitstreams/3c2989c4-688d-4d78-8a71-f02690990d51/content. Do not transfer its results directly to NQ: cash-equity opening auctions and futures overnight trading differ. A no-volume ORB is a separate weaker hypothesis."}
{"id":"hypothesis_C_indicator_map","topic":"realized_volatility_and_trend_pullback","content":"For volatility-expansion pullback hypothesis C, candidate features are: rolling 12-bar standard deviation of 5-minute log returns; a ratio to the historical mean volatility for the same clock window (computed only from earlier sessions); completed-hour EMA20 versus EMA60 as direction regime; and 5-minute EMA20 pullback/reclaim as entry condition. Compute hourly EMAs only from fully completed hourly bars. Andersen & Bollerslev, 'Intraday Periodicity and Volatility Persistence in Financial Markets,' Journal of Empirical Finance 4(2-3), 1997, 115-158, DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/S0927-5398(97)00004-2, shows intraday volatility periodicity affects high-frequency return dynamics in FX/equity markets; it supports time-of-day normalization, not this directional NQ setup. This pullback rule is exploratory and parameter-heavy; test its components via ablation and require fresh-data confirmation."}
{"id":"realized_semivariance_candidate","topic":"volatility_features","content":"Optional research feature after a simple realized-volatility baseline: split squared intraday returns into positive and negative realized semivariance to distinguish upside and downside variation, then test whether it changes momentum continuation/reversal outcomes. Liu, Lu, Li & Wang, 'Time series momentum and reversal: Intraday information from realized semivariance,' Journal of Empirical Finance 72, 2023, 54-77, DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jempfin.2023.03.001, studies commodity futures and reports out-of-sample results for its own strategy. It is not evidence for this exact NQ 5-minute implementation. Add only as one registered candidate after the simpler hypothesis is evaluated."}
{"id":"atr_and_ema_interpretation","topic":"indicator_roles","content":"ATR14 is useful as a volatility scale for stops, targets, and dimensionless returns (R); ATR by itself is not evidence of directional alpha. A 1x ATR stop and 2.2x ATR target specify barriers, not realized average loss/winner ratio: gaps, time exits, session flats, breakeven ratchets, and costs change realized outcomes. EMA20/EMA60 and stochastic/RSI/MFI are candidate feature transformations, not independently validated signals for this dataset. Test them as pre-registered ablations rather than stacking indicators until the backtest improves."}
{"id":"technical_rule_multiple_testing_evidence","topic":"indicator_evidence_and_overfitting","content":"Yen & Hsu, 'Profitability of technical analysis in financial and commodity futures markets - A reality check,' Decision Support Systems 50(1), 2010, 128-139, DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dss.2010.07.008, examined 8,061 rules across ten futures markets using superior-predictive-ability and related tests. The abstract reports the SPA results generally suggest market efficiency in nine of ten markets, while selected downside/upside Sortino results persisted in subsets. This is a warning that finding a good EMA/RSI/MFI parameter after many trials is not enough; record the full search and adjust for data snooping. The paper is not specific to NQ 5-minute bars."}
{"id":"data_requirements_for_indicators","topic":"required_data","content":"Minimum checks before testing the proposed features: several years of 5-minute OHLCV (volume is necessary for RVOL and VWAP); timestamps with an explicit timezone and correct daylight-saving treatment; hourly bars or a causal aggregation from 5-minute bars; dated contract identity or documented continuous-series roll construction; enough prior sessions to compute same-time-of-day baselines; and broker/exchange fees plus realistic slippage. The current notes report 6,532 labeled rows and 24-bar outcomes, which is not enough to establish the proposed >=200 out-of-sample-trade gate for low-frequency session strategies. Measure actual coverage from the data instead of assuming it."}
{"id":"research_to_model_pipeline","topic":"how_to_use_knowledge","content":"Use these documents as retrieved context for a research assistant to summarize hypotheses, data requirements, and test design. Do not concatenate article prose to every BUY/SELL/HOLD example or treat an article statement as a label. A robust pipeline is: retrieve relevant source note -> implement one frozen signal function -> generate causal labels or execute it directly in an event-driven backtest -> compare to no-trade, clock-matched, logistic-regression and gradient-boosted baselines -> evaluate net trades on purged chronological folds -> retain Qwen only if it adds stable out-of-sample net value at matched trade coverage. The assistant's text is advisory; only tested, versioned code and measured results enter the trading system."}
